WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.919
"The Best Way to Cook Vegetables"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.040
You may remember
when I compared the effects

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.080
of different cooking methods

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.480
on different phytonutrients
in bell peppers, mushrooms,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.600
and a few other veggies.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.360
Well, food scientists

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.200
have outdone themselves
in this study.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.120
They looked at six
different cooking methods

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.080
on 20 different vegetables,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.440
comparing three
distinct measures

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.640
of antioxidant activity.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.720
That's more than 300
separate experiments

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.000
to figure out the best way
to cook our vegetables.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.920
First, though,
let's figure out the worst

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.720
in terms of loss
of antioxidant content.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.280
Consider boiling, microwaving,
pressure-cooking in water,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.560
griddling, like cooking
in a frying pan without oil,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:43.840
frying in a frying pan,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.160
and baking at about 400 degrees
Fahrenheit (200°C).

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.800
What's your guess
for the worst?

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.800
The worst is boiling

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.400
when it comes
to losing antioxidants.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.520
The second worst?

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.120
Boiling under pressure.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.880
When we use
wet cooking methods,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.000
some of the nutrition is lost
into the cooking water;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.440
when it comes
to preparing vegetables

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.960
with their antioxidants
in mind,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.960
water is not
a cook's best friend.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.360
But how bad is bad?

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.680
It may be less than you think.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.760
Averaging
over those 20 vegetables,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.760
boiling only removes about
14 percent of the antioxidants.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.800
So, if you really like
boiled broccoli, fine,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.640
just eat one more floret.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.160
Seven florets
of boiled broccoli

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.520
has all the antioxidant power

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.520
of six florets
of raw broccoli.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.040
So, the best way
to eat your veggies

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.400
is really whichever way

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.800
will get you to eat
the most of them,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.320
with the exception of frying,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.840
which not only adds
empty calories

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.000
but also toxic byproducts
that are produced

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.960
when oils are heated
to frying temperatures.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.120
What's the gentlest
cooking method?

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.920
The one that preserves
the most antioxidants?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
Microwaving, which preserves
more than 97 percent

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.400
of the antioxidants.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.360
One vegetable has antioxidants
that can really get clobbered,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.480
no matter how you cook it,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.240
losing up to 75 percent
of its antioxidant capacity.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.600
What's the one vegetable
that's really best eaten raw?

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.160
Was it artichoke hearts,
asparagus, beets,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.400
broad beans
(also known as fava beans),

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.840
broccoli,

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.080
I hope we don't have to eat
raw Brussels sprouts,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.360
cauliflower, carrots, celery,
eggplant, garlic, green beans,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.080
leeks, corn on the cob,
onions, peas, bell peppers,

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.560
spinach, Swiss chard,
or zucchini?

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.800
The most vulnerable vegetable
is the bell pepper.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.120
So, I try to eat those raw
or add them right at the end

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.880
of whatever I'm cooking,
like pasta sauce,

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.720
so they really just
kind of get warmed up

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.640
without really being cooked.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.240
On the other hand,
three vegetables

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.000
are hardly affected
by cooking at all.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.840
You could even boil them
and still retain

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.280
nearly all their antioxidants.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.160
Can you guess
at least one of the three?

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.960
They're artichokes, beets,
and onions.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.160
Boil away.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.480
Asparagus gets
an honorable mention here.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.720
It's pretty much unaffected
by any cooking method

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.920
except frying,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.800
so you can boil asparagus, too.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.800
Final question, and perhaps
the most interesting,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.680
there are two vegetables that,
no matter what you do to them,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.400
tend to increase
in antioxidant value.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.000
They may become even healthier
upon cooking.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.040
Which two are they?

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.600
First, the runner-up:
green beans.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.520
With the exception of boiling
and pressure cooking,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.680
they actually increase
in antioxidant power

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.760
when cooked,
so microwaved green beans

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.920
have more antioxidants
than raw.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.560
And tied for first?

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.120
Which two vegetables
nearly always increase in value,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.800
no matter how you cook them?

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.640
Carrots and celery.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.560
So, when we make
a nice vegetable soup,

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.760
we can actually boost
their nutrition.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.200
One vegetable
that wasn't covered

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.120
was one of my favorites: kale.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.000
As I discuss in my video
How to Cook Greens,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.320
kale can be cooked
by blanching or steaming

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.120
to boost antioxidants.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.400
It can even be boiled

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.352
and not lose
antioxidant nutrition.

